WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:09.360
Brazil, the land of carnival, samba, soccer...&nbsp;
and oil. Indeed, VisualPolitik community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.160
Brazil is not only the cradle of the Amazon,&nbsp;
the lungs of the planet, but also one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.960
world's largest oil producers, as well as&nbsp;
the largest producer in all of Latin America.&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.920
In fact, in 2022 Brazil produced more than 4% of&nbsp;
all the oil extracted in the world. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.180
it is not surprising that it rubs shoulders with&nbsp;
major oil producers in the Middle East such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:35.460
Kuwait, Iran or the United Arab Emirates.
 
Even so, Brazil is not only constantly on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.360
the lips of analysts because of its strong&nbsp;
oil position. Less than a year ago, Latin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.030
America's largest economy made headlines&nbsp;
around the world because of this news:
 &nbsp;

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.800
(Brazil presidential election: Lula defeats&nbsp;
Bolsonaro by thin margin. – Irish Times)
 &nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.300
Indeed, Lula, who has been considered by many&nbsp;
as the rockstar of the left in Latin America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.320
returned to power by a very narrow margin&nbsp;
in the elections against the already former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.760
president Bolsonaro, Trump's ally who&nbsp;
also failed to extend his mandate.
 &nbsp;

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.620
And, as many of you will remember, this also&nbsp;
caused one of the most unfortunate incidents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.720
in Brazilian history: a bunch of citizens unhappy&nbsp;
with the outcome took a cue from the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.260
and stormed the nation's Congress.
 
However, they achieved little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.940
than being arrested and some were injured,&nbsp;
because the reality is that Lula had won,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.400
and in January of this year he assumed power.
 
Since then, Lula has emphasized social and, above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.740
all, environmental measures. Therefore, many fear&nbsp;
that with this third Lula presidency, the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.840
Brazilian oil has come. After all, that is exactly&nbsp;
the path followed by Gustavo Petro's Colombia.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.040
The point is that all this has made us on&nbsp;
VisualPolitik wonder about a few things:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.080
How did Brazil become an oil power? What role did&nbsp;
oil play in Lula's first two presidencies? Will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.060
Lula change his position on oil to comply with the&nbsp;
climate agenda? Will this be the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.980
end of Brazilian oil? Today we are going to answer&nbsp;
all these questions, but first let's take a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:08.640
at a bit of history.
 
(A LONG ROAD)
 &nbsp;

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:17.340
How long do you think it took Brazil to become&nbsp;
one of the major oil powers? I'm sure many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.720
you will think that as soon as oil was found,&nbsp;
money started pouring in from all directions.
 &nbsp;

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.980
But the reality is that, like Rome,&nbsp;
oil-producing Brazil was not built in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.480
day. The road to becoming one of the 10 largest&nbsp;
oil-producing powers was full of failures and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.400
above all, it was very long: more than a whole&nbsp;
century since its first oil exploration.
 &nbsp;

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.960
You see, after gaining independence in&nbsp;
1822, the first barrels of oil were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.900
extracted in Brazil until 1892 with Eugenio&nbsp;
Ferreire de Camargo, although only 2 barrels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.920
were extracted. Nevertheless, with this first&nbsp;
extraction, the Brazilian State realized the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.640
enormous potential of its subsoil and,&nbsp;
at the beginning of the 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.080
the public initiative joined the private&nbsp;
sector in the search for commercial oilfields:
 &nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.120
In 1907, the Brazilian government created the&nbsp;
Geological and Mineralogical Service of Brazil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.640
which drilled 60 wells in search of oil, although&nbsp;
it was not very successful. However, in 1941,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.840
with the help of the newly created National&nbsp;
Petroleum Council, the first commercial well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.000
was finally established in the municipalities&nbsp;
of Dom Joao and Agua Grande. But the country's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.440
production was still not abundant: By the&nbsp;
end of the 1940s, Brazil was only producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:28.020
2,700 barrels per day at a time when its&nbsp;
domestic demand was more than 170,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.980
barrels per day due to the industrialization&nbsp;
process the country was undergoing.
 &nbsp;

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:40.860
Nevertheless, this was to begin to change with&nbsp;
the birth of Brazil's major oil player. In 1953,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.860
the Brazilian government founded a&nbsp;
company that would shape the State's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:50.160
monopoly on oil research, drilling, refining&nbsp;
and transportation. Petróleo Brasileiro SA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.300
better known as Petrobras, was born.
Many of you already know that today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.380
Petrobras is Brazil's most important&nbsp;
company. But did you know that it spent its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:03.960
first 15 years of existence being almost totally&nbsp;
unproductive? The truth is, VisualPolitik viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.580
it was not until 1968 that Petrobras made&nbsp;
its first discovery: the Guaricema field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.600
although this would seem like nothing compared to&nbsp;
the two fields it discovered six years later in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.700
the Campos Basin: the Garoupa and Pargo fields.
 
Now, do you think that Brazil was all set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:21.300
to become the oil power it is today?&nbsp;
Nothing could be further from the truth.
 &nbsp;

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:30.600
By 1980, Brazilian production only covered&nbsp;
17% of domestic demand. Therefore, that same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:36.360
year, Joao Figuereido's government set a goal of&nbsp;
500,000 barrels per day of production by 1985.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.280
Yet it was not until the 1995 constitutional&nbsp;
reform that the Brazilian oil sector really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.360
began to bear fruit. This reform opened&nbsp;
the sector to private competition, lifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.920
restrictions on the entry of foreign capital and,&nbsp;
more importantly, ended the monopoly of Petrobras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.300
which became a semi-public company with the&nbsp;
majority of shares in the hands of the State.
 &nbsp;

00:04:54.300 --> 00:05:01.860
With this change, production increased and Brazil&nbsp;
achieved the longed-for oil self-sufficiency in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.280
the early 2000s. However, more than a&nbsp;
century had passed since that first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:11.160
small oil extraction in 1892, and Brazil was&nbsp;
still not in the top 10 of the great oil powers.
 &nbsp;

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.720
But that would change with the arrival of one of&nbsp;
the most significant figures in Latin American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:26.820
politics: Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva.
 
(LULA, THE LUCKY ONE)
 &nbsp;

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:33.960
In order for your name to go down in&nbsp;
politics history, it is often not about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.340
having the best managers, but it is also&nbsp;
about being lucky, and Lula, in his first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:42.480
two presidencies, was lucky. He certainly was.
 
You see, the Brazilian leader, who won his first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.980
presidential elections in 2002, managed to&nbsp;
lead Brazil through a period of prosperity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.540
not so much because of his impeccable management&nbsp;
of the economy, but above all because of several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:57.240
lucky breaks. The most important one? This one:
 
(Brazil discovers country's largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.460
oil field. – El Mundo)
 
Indeed, VisualPolitik viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:05.580
in 2007 one of the largest oil fields in the&nbsp;
country and in the world was discovered: the Tupi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:11.100
field. A field with reserves of about 29 billion&nbsp;
barrels of oil. This would finally place Brazil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:17.520
among the 10 largest oil producers in the world.
Now, Lula was no fool, the cost was not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.960
be low. In 2010 he passed laws so that part of&nbsp;
the companies' profits would go to the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.460
coffers. In addition, it is also true that he&nbsp;
benefited Petrobras, with which he signed an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.560
agreement allowing it to exploit and produce&nbsp;
up to 5 billion barrels of oil for 40 years.
 &nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.940
But even with all this, his luck did not end&nbsp;
there. Think about it, Lula came to power in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.900
a time of certain stability. His predecessor,&nbsp;
Cardoso, managed to tame inflation and avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.680
a major recession. But, most importantly&nbsp;
of all, Lula also came right in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.320
of the well-known commodity "super cycle", a&nbsp;
time when commodity prices were skyrocketing.&nbsp;

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:58.200
In fact, with the arrival of Lula, the trade&nbsp;
surplus went from $10 billion in 2002 to $47&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:04.260
billion in 2007 thanks to the discovery of the&nbsp;
oil field and the high costs of raw materials.
 &nbsp;

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:09.900
So, let's just say it's no wonder that the country&nbsp;
grew at an average of 4.5% per year during his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.400
two presidencies. And do you know what he did with&nbsp;
all the money he managed to raise? Well, in short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.900
he extended social programs. Or, to put it&nbsp;
another way, he enlarged the size of the State.
 &nbsp;

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.660
Lula launched programs such as Bolsa Familia,&nbsp;
which gave monthly aid to the poorest families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:30.120
or Hambre Zero, a campaign that aimed to reduce&nbsp;
the number of people who were going hungry. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:36.780
to be perfectly honest, they were a great success.
As you can see, the percentage of the Brazilian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.940
population in poverty fell by more than 15&nbsp;
percentage points between 2001 and 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:48.660
both because of the Bolsa Familia program which&nbsp;
benefited 12.4 million households, and because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:53.580
the increase in the employed population from&nbsp;
77 million to more than 90 million people.
 &nbsp;

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:57.600
And, not only that, Lula also began to promote&nbsp;
measures for the protection of the environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.640
particularly against the deforestation of the&nbsp;
Amazon. To give you an idea, the area deforested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:11.280
annually between 2000 and 2005 was 34,700 km2, an&nbsp;
area almost equivalent to Switzerland. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:17.280
from 2005 until the end of his presidency,&nbsp;
it was reduced to 4,500 km2 per year.
 &nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.880
At the time, Lula was tremendously&nbsp;
committed to the protection of the Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:26.280
but his fight against greenhouse gas emissions&nbsp;
caused by hydrocarbons such as oil and gas was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:32.820
not as strong. Why? For one simple reason: Lula&nbsp;
is what is known as an "oil nationalist". And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:37.440
what does this mean? Basically, he cannot&nbsp;
conceive of Brazil's progress without oil.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.000
Nevertheless, 13 years after the end of his&nbsp;
second presidency, Lula has returned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.660
political scene. It’s a very different political&nbsp;
scenario than the one he encountered previously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:50.340
for example: today the fight for the reduction of&nbsp;
greenhouse gas emissions is much more intense. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:54.360
you might ask, will Lula give in to the demands&nbsp;
of the climate agenda to the detriment of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:59.040
people? Could we be looking at the end of the&nbsp;
oil industry in Brazil? Well, let's take a look.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:05.520
(LULA'S DECISION)
 &nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:14.160
Lula's return to power was a relief for much&nbsp;
of the international community concerned about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:19.380
Bolsonaro's environmental management in Brazil.&nbsp;
It should be noted that he closed several nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.880
protection agencies, such as the Chico Mendes&nbsp;
Institute; he reduced spending on climate change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:30.450
mitigation policies by 93%, and deforestation was&nbsp;
rampant in several parts of the country, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:34.440
Cerrado, the Brazilian savannah. A place where&nbsp;
deforestation grew by 34% during his presidency.
 &nbsp;

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.580
Nevertheless, although many have rejoiced at&nbsp;
Lula's return, the reality is that the scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:43.740
is not as favorable as in his first cycle: the&nbsp;
Congress is dominated by bolsonarists, the effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:48.420
of the pandemic are still being felt, especially&nbsp;
with a debt that has reached 88% of GDP;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:54.720
poverty levels are still around 30% and interest&nbsp;
rates are stuck at 13.75%, something that has led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.760
Lula to an open confrontation with the Central&nbsp;
Bank. And, to top it all off, the country's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.900
growth projections are not the best either:
In fact, Brazil is expected to grow more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.020
half a point behind the rest of&nbsp;
Latin America and the Caribbean.
 &nbsp;

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.160
However, against all odds, the&nbsp;
Brazilian economy has been a surprise:
 &nbsp;

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:16.140
(Brazil's economy improves under&nbsp;
Lula, defies Central Bank rates&nbsp;

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.040
A rebound in Brazil's agriculture&nbsp;
and a strong labor market helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:26.160
deliver far better results than economists had&nbsp;
forecast at the start of 2023. – Bloomberg)
 &nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.680
In addition, during these first months of his&nbsp;
new presidency, Lula has promoted several reforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.420
reminiscent of his first two presidencies. For&nbsp;
example, he has reopened the Bolsa Familia program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:42.420
that Bolsonaro closed in 2021 and reinstated the&nbsp;
National Food Security Council. Meanwhile on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:47.760
environmental front, he reopened the Amazon Fund,&nbsp;
signed laws to seize illegally deforested land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.360
and reopened the Bolsa Verde program,&nbsp;
which gives money to poor families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.140
living in traditional communities who are&nbsp;
responsible for protecting the environment.
 &nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.080
In general, these measures contribute&nbsp;
to Brazil's repositioning as a leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:04.380
in the environmental struggle. Even&nbsp;
so, the question many are asking is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:10.260
is Lula willing to emulate Gustavo Petro, to go&nbsp;
one step further and do away with national oil?
 &nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.560
Well, the truth is that the chances&nbsp;
of Lula sacrificing the Brazilian oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:18.000
industry to achieve this goal are&nbsp;
rather low, if not non-existent.
 &nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.580
(Lula described Gustavo Petro's proposal&nbsp;
to suspend oil production as "unreal".&nbsp;

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:29.040
Former Brazilian president Luiz Inácio Lula&nbsp;
da Silva assured that the use of hydrocarbons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.600
is still necessary in the world and gave as an&nbsp;
example the situation in Germany. – InfoBae)
 &nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:37.800
In fact, if Lula has already managed to place&nbsp;
Brazil among the world's top 10 oil producers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:42.540
his goal now is to make it the world's&nbsp;
fourth largest producer. For this reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:47.640
Petrobras expects to increase daily production&nbsp;
from 3 to 5 million barrels by 2030.
 &nbsp;

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:52.260
And, not only that, the Brazilian oil giant is&nbsp;
also trying to get a license to be able to drill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:57.420
in the Amazon basin, in an area where there are&nbsp;
estimated reserves of up to 30 billion barrels of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:03.120
oil and where it is willing to invest $6 billion&nbsp;
in exploration alone over the next five years.&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:10.200
Added to this is the fact&nbsp;
that, between 2022 and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:13.200
Brazil has approved the largest&nbsp;
number of oil and gas projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:17.760
behind only Saudi Arabia and Qatar.
 
And this is not so unusual, just remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:22.500
that projections indicate that Petrobras will&nbsp;
provide more than $200 billion in revenues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:27.780
the State coffers in the next five years.&nbsp;
$200 billion… that Lula is counting on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.780
carry out his social policies.
 
You see VisualPolitik community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:34.920
it does not appear that Brazil is going to break&nbsp;
with its oil tradition with Lula. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:39.480
it seems that exactly the opposite will happen,&nbsp;
his oil gamble will become even stronger.

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:43.500
Yes, it is true, he has restored initiatives&nbsp;
to fight against factors contributing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:48.540
climate change, such as deforestation, but oil&nbsp;
exploitation in Brazil seems non-negotiable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:51.960
especially if Lula wants to maintain&nbsp;
his social spending. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:53.760
the environment is one of Lula's priorities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:58.620
but, at the end of the day, petrodollars&nbsp;
continue to dominate the scale of priorities.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:03.120
But, at this point, the questions are now over&nbsp;
to you: do you think Lula will ultimately reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.320
the influence of the oil industry in Brazil?&nbsp;
What position did you think Lula had on the oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.400
industry? You can leave me your answer in the&nbsp;
comments. As always, don't forget that here on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:15.660
VisualPolitik we release new videos every week, so&nbsp;
subscribe to this channel and hit the little bell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:19.740
so you don't miss any of our updates. If you liked&nbsp;
this video, LIKE it and see you in the next one.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:22.860
All the best and see you soon.

